# modelguild.conf.example — copy to modelguild/modelguild.conf.local (git-ignored) to set your
# persistent ModelGuild preferences, or run /guild:configure, which writes it
# for you. Simple KEY=value lines; `#` starts a comment; this file is NOT executed.
#
# Why a file and not environment variables: this tool is driven by Claude Code, whose
# shell runs each command in a subshell — so a setup command can't `export` a value
# into your interactive shell and have it persist. A git-ignored file can. The env
# vars still work as one-off overrides (precedence: explicit arg > env > this file).
#
# Values here are read by the guild MCP tools (GUILD_MODEL for single-model calls,
# GUILD_MODELS for the panel). Model policy (deny/ask/allow) lives separately in the
# models.policy files -- see modelguild/models.policy for that chain.
#
# LAYERED (issue #19): this file is read from BOTH roots. The global
# ~/.claude/modelguild/modelguild.conf.local is the BASELINE and a project's own
# <repo>/modelguild/modelguild.conf.local is overlaid ON TOP of it, key by key: a key
# set in the project WINS, a key the project leaves unset falls THROUGH to global. So
# set your defaults once globally and only override per project what differs.
# $GUILD_CONF, when set, is a single-FILE override -- it replaces the whole chain.
# See /guild:configure, which asks which root to write.

# Default model for /guild:consult (and any single-model call). Prefer a NON-Claude model
# so a second opinion is genuinely independent.
# GUILD_MODEL=openai/gpt-5

# Default panel for /guild:panel: 2-3 ordered ids from DIFFERENT providers (space- or
# comma-separated). guild_panel warns if the set isn't cross-provider.
# GUILD_MODELS=openai/gpt-5 google/gemini-2.5-pro

# Per-model-turn HTTP timeout in milliseconds. Default 900000 (15 min). RAISE this when a
# heavy task on a slow reasoning model legitimately runs long and aborts with "operation
# was aborted due to timeout" — e.g. a whole-repo review or a long planning session. Only
# the model-turn call uses it; the fast control-plane calls keep their own short timeout.
# Must be a positive number of milliseconds; 0/negative/non-numeric falls back to the
# default (there is no "disable timeout" — a hung turn must eventually abort). Capped at
# 2147483647 (2^31-1), Node's timer ceiling — a larger value would abort every turn instantly.
# Set it to the literal `max` to use that ceiling (~24.8 days, "effectively never abort");
# the trade-off is a genuinely hung turn then blocks until then. This sets the DEFAULT; the
# calling agent can also override it per call via each tool's `timeoutMs` input (which wins).
# GUILD_MESSAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000

# Where the guild tools look for the guild-* agent DEFS when deciding whether to refuse
# a call whose hardened def is missing. Default: the .opencode/agent of the project the
# MCP server was spawned from (GUILD_PROJECT_DIR, else the CWD). NOTE: this governs ONLY
# the tool's refusal decision — opencode resolves --agent independently from its own
# config, so this MUST point at the SAME directory the defs were actually installed to.
# GUILD_AGENT_DIR=~/.config/opencode/agent

# The guild root (where the policy/config/logs live). LAYERED: <cwd>/modelguild/ over
# ~/.claude/modelguild/, most-specific first, both binding. $GUILD_ROOT overrides that
# with a SINGLE root -- nothing is layered under it, so your global policy and
# preferences stop binding. Set it only for a fixture, a CI sandbox, or a genuinely
# non-standard layout. Writes (the evidence logs/) always go to the most-specific root.
# GUILD_ROOT=/path/to/modelguild

# --- Evidence layer (src/log.ts) ------------------------------------------------
# Every model call is recorded to the git-ignored modelguild/logs/<run_id>/calls.jsonl:
# raw prompts and responses, with `expected-call`, `started`, and `completed`
# lifecycle entries sharing one call_id. These are the RECEIPTS of every exchange —
# the other model's actual words, on disk and yours to read — so logging is on by default.
# GUILD_LOG=off                  # stop recording entirely
# GUILD_LOG_DIR=/custom/path     # override the log root (disables partitioning below)
# GUILD_LOG_PROMPTS=full         # full | hash | off — what to keep of the prompt
#                                 # Claude SENT. Default `full`: the brief Claude
#                                 # wrote is itself prime audit material. But it
#                                 # means the log holds whatever repo context was
#                                 # pasted in — use `hash` (digest only, proves the
#                                 # prompt didn't change) or `off` on sensitive work.
#                                 # Responses are ALWAYS kept in full: they are the
#                                 # evidence, and they're the other model's words.
# GUILD_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=14    # prune runs older than this; 0 disables pruning.
#                                 # Applied when the MCP server starts and at each new
#                                 # run; `npx modelguild logs clean [--dry-run]` applies
#                                 # it on demand. A run's age is its NEWEST content, so a
#                                 # long-lived run still being written to is never pruned
#                                 # out from under itself. A value that isn't a whole
#                                 # number disables pruning rather than falling back to
#                                 # 14 — a typo must never be a licence to delete.
#                                 # Layered like every key here (a global value binds in
#                                 # a project that doesn't restate it), but only the
#                                 # most-specific root's logs/ is pruned — that is the
#                                 # only root runs are ever written to.
# GUILD_LOG_PARTITION=1          # opt-in (default OFF). Narrow the log root to a
#                                 # per-project subdir BELOW the base logs tree:
#                                 # <base>/<project-key>/<run>, keyed by the CWD's git
#                                 # top-level (else the CWD). Keeps each project's runs,
#                                 # `latest` symlink, and retention separate under a
#                                 # single shared install. DISABLED when GUILD_LOG_DIR
#                                 # is explicitly set (an explicit dir wins). For a
#                                 # NON-repo project the key uses $PWD (logical), so
#                                 # entering the same dir via different symlink paths
#                                 # yields two buckets — bounded, not corruption.

# --- Live activity (src/activity.ts) --------------------------------------------
# While a guild call runs, the server subscribes to opencode's event stream and records
# what the external model is DOING — the files it read, what it grepped, fetched and
# edited, the shell commands it ran — line by line to
# modelguild/logs/<run_id>/activity.jsonl, as it happens. Tail it live with
# `npx modelguild watch`; every tool result also carries a bounded summary. This is
# visibility, NOT containment: seeing a command scroll past does not gate it, and the
# delegate diff review is still the review point. It is also not the RECEIPTS — the
# model's actual words stay in calls.jsonl; these lines are opencode's report of the
# model's actions, at opencode's fidelity. On by default: a stream you have to remember
# to turn on before a 15-minute delegate call is a stream you will not have.
# GUILD_ACTIVITY=off             # stop watching entirely: no event subscription is
#                                 # opened, no activity.jsonl, no summary on the result.
#                                 # GUILD_LOG=off silences it too — with no run directory
#                                 # there is nowhere to write, and turning the record off
#                                 # must not leave a different record behind.
# GUILD_ACTIVITY_DETAIL=summary  # summary | full — how much of each event to keep.
#                                 # Default `summary`: the tool name plus a TRUNCATED
#                                 # rendering of its input (the path read, the command
#                                 # run). `full` also records each event's raw properties,
#                                 # which can carry tool OUTPUT — that is file contents
#                                 # the model read, landing on your disk. Same sensitivity
#                                 # trade-off as GUILD_LOG_PROMPTS=full: reach for it when
#                                 # you are debugging what a model did, not as a standing
#                                 # default on sensitive work.

# --- Approval bridge (src/approve.ts) -------------------------------------------
# OPT-IN, OFF BY DEFAULT. Watching is not gating: the activity stream above shows you what
# the model DID. This makes it ask BEFORE it acts, by moving the chosen tools to `ask` for
# that one session (the hardened agent defs are never modified) and routing each request to
# you. It is what issue #20 called the write-path approval bridge.
#
# THE HONEST BOUND, because it decides whether this is worth turning on: APPROVING `bash`
# APPROVES A SHELL. The prompt shows you a command string; that command can read any file
# (including your credentials), reach the network, and start processes that raise no further
# prompts. This buys ATTENTION and an INTERRUPT — it is NOT containment, and the delegate
# diff review remains the review point. Anyone reading an approval prompt as a guarantee has
# been misled.
#
# YOU MUST BE ABLE TO ANSWER, OR THE CALL IS REFUSED UP FRONT. Run `npx modelguild watch
# --approve` in another terminal (same project, logging on) before the call, or use an MCP
# client that can prompt you itself. With neither, the tool refuses instead of arming —
# because under `opencode serve` an unanswered permission request does NOT fail closed, it
# HANGS the turn until the model-turn timeout. A refusal naming the fix beats a 15-minute
# silence. A headless run (`claude -p`) auto-cancels its own prompt within milliseconds; that
# cancel DEFERS to a live watch terminal when there is one (so it cannot pre-empt your
# window) and REJECTS when it is the only channel. Either way a cancel is never consent.
#
# WHAT GETS GATED IS THE INTERSECTION with the agent def's own allow-set, read from the def
# in force at call time. So hardening a def further (denying `bash` in your own
# guild-build.md, say) also shrinks what the bridge will ever ask you about — it can never
# put a tool your def denies in front of you as an approvable one. If the def cannot be
# parsed, the call is refused rather than run ungated.
# GUILD_APPROVE=write            # off (default) | write | all
#                                 # write = gate edit/write/patch on /guild:delegate.
#                                 # all   = also gate bash (see the honest bound above).
#                                 # A typo here is an ERROR that refuses the call, NOT a
#                                 # silent fall to `off` — believing your edits are gated
#                                 # when they are not is the worst failure this can have.
# GUILD_APPROVE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000 # Unanswered ⇒ REJECT (fail-closed). Default 120000 (2 min).
#                                 # The model is told why, verbatim, and continues — a
#                                 # rejection is a denied tool call, not an aborted turn. Two
#                                 # costs: a rejected mid-sequence edit can leave a PARTIAL
#                                 # change set (still captured, still recoverable via
#                                 # capture.recoveryHint), and the wait is spent inside the
#                                 # SAME GUILD_MESSAGE_TIMEOUT_MS budget as the model turn.
# GUILD_APPROVE_EGRESS=ask        # off (default) | ask. SEPARATE knob, also opt-in: put
#                                 # webfetch/websearch behind the same prompt on the READ
#                                 # paths (guild-read/guild-research), i.e. /guild:consult,
#                                 # /guild:panel, /guild:research and friends.
#                                 # PROVENANCE, because this one is an asymmetry: the read
#                                 # paths are deliberately NOT fenced (the 2026-07-22
#                                 # realignment removed the secret-glob and grep/glob denials
#                                 # as vendor bias). The one ratified harness difference that
#                                 # survives is that a read on those paths EGRESSES to a
#                                 # third-party model provider, whereas a Claude subagent's
#                                 # read stays inside Anthropic. This knob is the single
#                                 # place that difference buys something — and it buys an
#                                 # OFFERED, OPT-IN gate, never a fence. Nothing is removed
#                                 # from the read paths, and the default is off.
#                                 # Maintainer decision, 2026-07-28 (design open question 8).
